## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design scalable I/O budgeting, cross-CPU coordination, and NUMA-aware fairness under heavy multi-tenant workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Per-VM isolation and quotas
- NUMA-aware accounting
- Token-bucket throttling
- Cross-CPU coordination via IPI
- CAS-based atomic updates

## Code Example

```c
struct vm_budget {
    atomic_long budget_bytes;
    atomic_long tokens;
    int numa_node;
};

void refill(struct vm_budget *vb) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&vb->tokens, REFILL_TOKEN);
}

static bool try_consume(struct vm_budget *vb, long cost) {
    long old, new;
    do {
        old = atomic_load(&vb->tokens);
        if (old < cost)
            return false;
        new = old - cost;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&vb->tokens, &old, new));
    return true;
}

void submit_io(struct vm_budget *vb, struct bio *bio) {
    if (!try_consume(vb, bio->size))
        enqueue_throttled(vb->numa_node, bio);
    else
        dispatch_io(bio);
}
```

## Minimal Patch Sketch

1. Add `struct vm_budget` to VM struct, indexed by NUMA node
2. Hook token consumption into `blk_mq_submit_bio()`
3. Per-CPU refill timer via `hrtimer`
4. Throttle path wakes remote CPUs via IPI when budget replenishes
5. Tune `REFILL_TOKEN` and queue limits via sysfs

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical synchronization primitives in a kernel-like setting, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Wait queues, locks, memory ordering
- Spurious wakeups and rechecking condition
- Correct wakeup under contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct waiter { struct waiter *next; task_t *t; } waiter_t;

typedef struct wq { spinlock_t lock; waiter_t *head; bool *flag; } wq_t;

void wait_on(wq_t *q, bool *flag){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  while (!*flag){
    enqueue(q, current); // add to wait list
    release(&q->lock);
    schedule();
    acquire(&q->lock);
  }
  release(&q->lock);
}

void wake_up(wq_t *q){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  *q->flag = true;
  wake_all(q); // wake all waiters
  release(&q->lock);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to handle timeouts?
- How to prevent missed wakeups when wake happens before wait is enqueued?
- Compare to condition variables vs. spin-wait approaches.

